#!ipxe

# ===========================================================================
# Example Music Limited -- Enterprise Network Boot
# Safe default: boot local disk after 30 seconds
#
# Boot server topology (bootstrap-only helpers -- IP only, no formal hostname):
#   192.168.139.50    Edinburgh datacentre  (gateway .254)
#   172.16.124.1:8000 Fredericia datacentre (gateway .2)
#                     Legal fiction -- physically a MacBook running
#                     python3 -m http.server 8000, mirroring /debian
#                     from Edinburgh. Only the IP differs.
#
# boot-url is set dynamically by gateway detection. To add a new environment:
# add one iseq line and set boot-url. Nothing else needs changing.
# ===========================================================================
#
# Version history:
#
#   v2.11  :klargoring now supports arm64, following PVE 9.2's own arm64 release
#          (https://pve.proxmox.com/wiki/Roadmap, Robert, 2026-08-06). klargoring's real
#          GitHub release (github.com/knightmare2600/klargoring) publishes asymmetric
#          filenames per arch (amd64: vmlinuz/installer-initrd.img, arm64: vmlinuz-arm64/
#          installer-initrd-arm64.img) -- confirmed against the actual
#          klargoring-20260806-trixie-4 release, not guessed. Split into :klargoring-amd64/
#          :klargoring-arm64 sublabels with fully-literal paths rather than ${arch}-style
#          variable indirection, so check_bootstrap_assets.py's indirection resolver (not
#          per-branch-scoped) can't cross-product three independent variables into asset
#          paths iPXE would never actually request. Dropped the "(x86_64 only)" item label.
#
#   v2.10  Removed Ubuntu (:ubuntu-*, Alpine dd-writer pattern), Rocky Linux (:rocky-*), and
#          Arch Linux (:arch-*) entirely -- Robert's explicit call, 2026-07-27: none of these
#          were real/working (Ubuntu/Rocky had no committed assets at all; Arch's own
#          airootfs.sfs/initramfs-linux were still literal placeholder stub files, not real
#          assets -- see docs/bootstrap/bootstrapping.md's asset table). bootstrap/web/arch/,
#          bootstrap/web/rocky/install.ks, bootstrap/web/build-ubuntu-writer-apkovl.sh, and
#          docs/arch-pxe-setup.md all deleted alongside this. Also fixed :hdt -- it was
#          chaining to pxelinux.0, which expects TFTP for its own follow-up fetches (this
#          server is HTTP-only, no TFTP daemon anywhere in this repo, so this was never going
#          to work); switched to lpxelinux.0, SYSLINUX's HTTP-native PXELINUX build, fetched
#          via benarbejde/asset_manifest.json alongside hdt.c32/libcom32.c32/libutil.c32.
#
#   v2.9   Added :3cx-install (3CX Phone System, translated from bootstrap/web/3cx/boot.txt's
#          isolinux APPEND syntax) and inserted trixie/ into every :debian-* kernel/initrd
#          path, matching bootstrap/web/debian/'s split into trixie/+bookworm/ subfolders --
#          3CX's own installer kernel turned out to be genuine unmodified Debian 12
#          (Bookworm), confirmed byte-identical to a known snapshot.debian.org build, so it
#          lives at debian/bookworm/x86_64/linux rather than being duplicated under 3cx/.
#
#   v2.8   Bumped OpenBSD 7.5 -> 7.9 (all three :openbsd-* entries). 7.5 no longer exists on
#          cdn.openbsd.org at all (confirmed live, 2026-07-27, while building
#          benarbejde/asset_manifest.json's fetch-on-demand entries) -- the mirror only carries
#          7.7/7.8/7.9. Robert's explicit call to bump rather than chase the archive mirror
#          for a version this stale.
#
#   v2.7   Added :klargoring -- Projekt klargoring, Robert's own hand-rolled live Proxmox
#          installer distro, built separately from this repo's own automation.
#          NOT a revival of the Proxmox-official auto-install PXE approach
#          :proxmox-ve's own history (v2.3-v2.5 below) already tried and
#          abandoned after a failed live test -- different distro, different
#          mechanism, confirmed by Robert directly (2026-07-23). Wired to use
#          ${boot-url}/${site-prefix} like every other entry rather than the
#          hardcoded IP/VRK originally given, matching this file's own
#          established per-site answer-file convention.
#
#   v2.6   Dropped the EXAPRVVRK001/EXAPRVFRD001 hostname labels from the topology comment
#          and the two env_* echo lines -- both provisioning servers are bootstrap-only
#          helpers, deliberately given no formal EXA<ROLE><SITE><NNN> hostname or DNS record
#          any more (2026-07-21, Robert). IP only, same as boot-url already was.
#
#   v2.5   PROXMOX VE reduced to a stub, matching PROXMOX DCM's existing pattern.
#          v2.4's BMC-delivery approach was tested live 2026-07-18 and failed outright
#          (blank cursor -- the installer never picked up the BMC-mounted ISO the way a
#          network-loaded one did during earlier testing). Rather than debug an unattended
#          PXE flow further for something built as rarely as a PVE node, reverted to the
#          simpler, already-documented manual path (docs/bootstrap/bootstrapping.md 6.3):
#          mount a plain Proxmox ISO via iLO/BMC, pick Automated Install with no fetch URL
#          (drops to a root shell, expected behaviour), run the new
#          bootstrap/web/proxmox/select-pve-answer.sh there. Removed the now-dead
#          ${proxmox-ver} variable (was only ever used by the submenu this replaces).
#          Proxmox VE is not a PXE boot target at all any more -- see
#          docs/proxmox/pxe-proxmox-autoinstall-build-log.md for the full history,
#          including the abandoned approach.
#
#   v2.4   PROXMOX VE section corrected against docs/proxmox/pxe-proxmox-autoinstall-
#          build-log.md, a real working session against proxmox-auto-install-assistant.
#          proxmox-auto-install-mode=/proxmox-auto-install-url= kernel parameters (added
#          in v2.3, modelled on Debian's url= preseed convention) confirmed NOT real --
#          absent from the tool's own generated boot.ipxe, its --help, and the vanilla
#          ISO's initrd contents -- removed from :proxmox-auto. Dropped the second
#          initrd ... proxmox.iso HTTP fetch from :proxmox-load entirely -- BMC virtual
#          media is the confirmed, decided delivery method for the install payload and
#          per-site answer-file URL (baked into the mounted ISO at build time via
#          `prepare-iso`), not a kernel-line concern at all. Added an echo reminder at
#          the :proxmox-ve entry point to confirm the right ISO is mounted via iLO/BMC
#          before continuing, since this menu has no visibility into that. ${proxmox-ver}
#          kept as a display-only label (vmlinuz/initrd.img carry no version string and
#          are identical across every site/mode). Not yet tested end to end on real
#          hardware -- see the build log's own "Still open" section.
#
#   v2.3   PROXMOX VE section replaced entirely: the old extracted-kernel approach
#          (linux26/initrd via pve-iso-2-pxe.sh) is out, Proxmox's own modern
#          ISO-based auto-install boot is in -- vmlinuz/initrd.img plus the
#          installer ISO loaded as a named initrd module. Now a 7-mode submenu
#          (Automated/Graphical/Terminal UI/Serial/Debug/Debug TUI/Serial Debug),
#          matching Proxmox's own generated iPXE menu, adapted to use ${boot-url}/
#          ${arch}/${site-prefix} like every other section instead of the hardcoded
#          values a bare Proxmox-generated menu ships with. New ${proxmox-ver}
#          variable (set once, near the top) is the one place to bump on a point
#          release. proxmox-auto-install-url= carried over into the Automated
#          entry to keep the existing per-site answer-file mechanism working --
#          NOT YET CONFIRMED against this specific ISO variant, see the section's
#          own comment.
#
#   v2.2   lvm-bios.seed/lvm-efi.seed moved here from the separate bootstrap/debian/ staging
#          dir in git -- they were never actually copied to this served location, so
#          debian-auto/-ssh/-auto-s/-ssh-s would have 404'd on ${seed} if used. Added
#          debian-test as a deliberately separate, clearly-labelled entry to verify the fix
#          before relying on the four pre-existing entries again.
#
#   v2.0   Gateway-based boot-url detection. Removes hardcoded IP throughout.
#          Edinburgh (gw 192.168.139.254) or Fredericia (gw 172.16.124.2).
#          Fix archiso_http_srv was hardcoded, now uses ${boot-url}. Fix WinPE
#          ARM64; uefi-http-boot-url now references ${boot-url}. One variable,
#          one place to change.
#
#   v2.1   Rewrite WinPE entries. ARM64 now uses wimboot.arm64 (added in wimboot
#          v2.8.0). Same wimboot streaming pattern as x86_64, no UEFI HTTP Boot
#          handoff. x86_64: UEFI firmware, & wimboot/BIOS firmware wimboot.i386;
#          detected via build arch. Both stream a single self-contained boot.wim
#          as wimboot 2.7.1+ extracts BCD & boot.sdi from WIM automatically with
#          wimboot 2.9.0 also extracting boot.stl for Windows 11 24H2. Recommend
#          upgrading all wimboot binaries to v2.9.0.
#
#   v1.9   WinPE x86_64 entry switched to wimboot.i386 (BIOS binary) For wimboot
#          (EFI binary) caused CPU hang on legacy BIOS VMs.
#          BCD in boot\ uses winload.exe; BCD.efi uses winload.efi.
#          Both files are present in the iPXE folder for future reference.
#
#   v1.8   Replace PhoenixPE entry with lean custom WinPE build.
#            x86_64: wimboot streaming from HTTP as before.
#            ARM64:  UEFI HTTP Boot path (wimboot does not support ARM64).
#          Both WIMs include VMware PVSCSI/VMXNET3 & VirtIO SCSI/NetKVM drivers
#          Boot target is cmd.exe; tools in Windows\Tools\ directory.
#
#   v1.7   Switch Alpine helper from virt to LTS kernel. Add storage drivers to
#          to modules= kernel parameter so initramfs loads them before the local
#          service runs, apkovl to use depmod -a rather than apk add linux-virt
#          (which caused ENOSPC). Add GB keymap via 00-keymap.start. Fix rm -rf
#          to unlink symlink first.
#
#   v1.6   Pin alpine_repo = v3.23 for Ubuntu boots. latest-stable causes kernel
#          version mismatch between running kernel & available modules. Rebuild
#          apkovl to pin /etc/apk/repositories to v3.23 and install linux-virt
#          inside writer script to get matching storage modules.
#
#   v1.5   Replace Ubuntu Subiquity live install with Alpine dd-writer pattern.
#          Alpine virt netboot (~50 MB) streams the Ubuntu 24.04 minimal cloud
#          image (~247 MB) onto disk via wget|dd, skipping the 1.1GB Subiquity
#          squashfs entirely.
#          cloud-init nocloud seed served from ${boot-url}/ubuntu/.
#          Add alpine-arch remap (arm64->aarch64) matching rocky-arch pattern.
#   v1.4   Restore inst.stage2= in Rocky entries, pointing at minimal boot.iso
#          images/install.img on Rocky CDN (~200 MB, not ~700 MB). Add per-entry
#          rocky-arch remap (iPXE uses arm64, Rocky mirror uses aarch64) so CDN
#          URL resolves correctly on both arches.
#
#   v1.3   Rename file from boot.ipxe to menu.ipxe to match server layout
#
#   v1.2   Remove inst.stage2= from all Rocky entries; stage2 now resolved from
#          url= in install.ks, preventing ramdisk overflow at boot
#
#   v1.1   Fix missing Ubuntu & OpenBSD menu items; OpenBSD after Arch Linux,
#          before Hypervisors & Ubuntu placed after Debian.
#
#   v1.0   Initial release
#
# ===========================================================================

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Make the hostname traceable e.g. ipxe-00-0c-29-be-ef-69
set hostname ipxe-${net0/mac:hexhyp}
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# For better hallway vision - (C) John Bender Saturday March 24th 1984
#console --x 1024 --y 768 --depth 32
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Architecture normalisation
#   iPXE buildarch values:  i386 (BIOS x86)  x86_64 (UEFI x86)  arm64 (UEFI ARM)
#   We normalise i386 -> x86_64 so payload paths are consistent.
#   Result: ${arch} is always either x86_64 or arm64.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
iseq ${buildarch} arm64 && set arch arm64 || set arch x86_64
echo Detected: ${buildarch} -- payload arch: ${arch}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Preseed selection by architecture
#    arm64 -> lvm-efi.seed  (EFI recipe, arm64 kernel, no biosgrub partition)
#   x86_64 -> lvm-bios.seed (BIOS/GPT recipe, amd64 kernel, biosgrub partition)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
iseq ${arch} arm64 && set seed lvm-efi.seed || set seed lvm-bios.seed
echo Preseed: ${seed}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Gateway-based environment detection
# Sets ${boot-url} and ${site-prefix} once; everything below uses them exclusively.
# ${site-prefix} matches this site's own devices.csv/sites.csv Site code, and selects
# which server-specific Proxmox answer file to request (VRK-answer.toml, FRD-answer.toml,
# etc -- see bootstrap/web/proxmox/*.toml). Fixed 2026-07-11: previously only ${boot-url}
# was site-aware, so every site fetched the exact same literal "answer.toml", which was
# only ever correct for Edinburgh (TOML can't do this gateway-based branching itself --
# see VRK-answer.toml's own comment). To add a new environment: one iseq line, set
# boot-url, set site-prefix -- nothing else needs changing.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
echo Detecting boot environment from gateway ${net0/gateway}...
iseq ${net0/gateway} 192.168.139.254 && goto env_edinburgh  ||
iseq ${net0/gateway} 172.16.124.2    && goto env_fredericia ||
goto env_fallback

:env_edinburgh
echo Environment: Edinburgh (192.168.139.50)
set boot-url http://192.168.139.50
set site-prefix VRK
goto env_done

:env_fredericia
echo Environment: Fredericia (172.16.124.1 - MacBook/Fusion NAT)
set boot-url http://172.16.124.1:8000
set site-prefix FRD
goto env_done

:env_fallback
echo Gateway ${net0/gateway} not recognised -- defaulting to Edinburgh
set boot-url http://192.168.139.50
set site-prefix VRK

:env_done
echo Boot server: ${boot-url}  (site-prefix: ${site-prefix})
echo

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Auto-deploy: chain to a MAC-specific script if one exists, else menu
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
chain --autofree ${boot-url}/autodeploy/${mac:hexhyp}.ipxe || goto main

# ===========================================================================
# MAIN MENU
# ===========================================================================
:main
menu Example Music: Enterprise Network Boot  Arch: [ ${arch} ]
item --gap --
item --gap --         Hostname: ${hostname}  /  MAC: ${mac}
item --gap --         Host IP : ${net0/ip}   / Boot URL: ${boot-url}
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- Default Selection --
item localboot        Boot local disk  (default, 30s timeout)
item spejder          Spejder Hardware Provisioning Runtime (Console and Serial ttyS0/COM1)
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- Debian --
item debian-auto      Debian  Auto install
item debian-ssh       Debian  Auto install  (SSH console)
item debian-auto-s    Debian  Auto install  (ttyS0 serial)
item debian-ssh-s     Debian  Auto install  (SSH + ttyS0 serial)
item debian-test      Debian  [TEST] seed file relocation fix -- try this first
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- 3CX Phone System --
item 3cx-install      3CX Phone System  Auto install  (Debian Bookworm base)
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- OpenBSD --
item openbsd-auto     OpenBSD 7.9  Auto install
item openbsd-auto-s   OpenBSD 7.9  Auto install  (ttyS0 serial)
item openbsd-inter-s  OpenBSD 7.9  Interactive   (ttyS0 serial)
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- Hypervisors --
item proxmox-ve       Proxmox VE 9   (x86_64 only)
item proxmox-dcm      Proxmox DCM 9  (x86_64 only)
item klargoring             Projekt klargoring  (custom live Proxmox installer, x86_64 or ARM64)
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- Utilities --
item gparted          GParted Live
item winpe-x64        WinPE deployment environment  (x86_64)
item winpe-arm64      WinPE deployment environment  (ARM64)
item hdt              Hardware Detection Tool  (x86_64 only)
item --gap --
item --gap --         -- System --
item shell            iPXE shell
item reboot           Reboot
item shutdown         Shutdown

choose --default localboot --timeout 30000 target || goto localboot
goto ${target}

# ===========================================================================
# LOCAL BOOT
# ===========================================================================
:localboot
sanboot --no-describe --drive 0x80 || exit

:spejder
kernel ${boot-url}/spejder/${arch}/linux-${arch} console=ttyS0,115200 console=tty0 rw net.ifnames=0 biosdevname=0 quiet loglevel=3
initrd ${boot-url}/spejder/${arch}/initramfs-${arch}.cpio.zst
boot

# ===========================================================================
# DEBIAN
# Common kernel parameters factored into comments for reference:
#   auto=true url=... priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb
#   domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false
#   timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London
#   netcfg/choose_interface=auto
# ===========================================================================
:debian-auto
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/linux auto=true url=${boot-url}/debian/${seed} priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London netcfg/choose_interface=auto
initrd ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/initrd.gz
boot

:debian-ssh
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/linux auto=true url=${boot-url}/debian/${seed} priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London netcfg/choose_interface=auto anna/choose_modules=network-console network-console/password=install network-console/password-again=install
initrd ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/initrd.gz
boot

:debian-auto-s
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/linux auto=true url=${boot-url}/debian/${seed} priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London netcfg/choose_interface=auto console=ttyS0,115200n8
initrd ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/initrd.gz
boot

:debian-ssh-s
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/linux auto=true url=${boot-url}/debian/${seed} priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London netcfg/choose_interface=auto anna/choose_modules=network-console network-console/password=install network-console/password-again=install console=tty0 console=ttyS0,115200n8
initrd ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/initrd.gz
boot

# 2026-07-08: lvm-bios.seed/lvm-efi.seed were missing from this served
# location entirely until just now (only existed in the separate
# bootstrap/debian/ staging dir in git, never copied over) -- meaning
# debian-auto/-ssh/-auto-s/-ssh-s above would have 404'd on ${seed} if
# actually used before this fix. This entry is a deliberately separate,
# clearly-labelled test of the fix -- pick this one first; the four above
# are untouched and should also now work identically once this is confirmed.
:debian-test
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/linux auto=true url=${boot-url}/debian/${seed} priority=high locale=en_GB.UTF-8 keymap=gb domain=jukebox.internal theme=dark lowmem/low=false timezone=Europe/London time/zone=Europe/London netcfg/choose_interface=auto
initrd ${boot-url}/debian/trixie/${arch}/initrd.gz
boot

# ===========================================================================
# 3CX PHONE SYSTEM
# 3CX's own official netboot recipe (bootstrap/web/3cx/boot.txt, vendor-supplied
# alongside this build), translated from isolinux APPEND syntax into iPXE's
# separate kernel/initrd commands -- the underlying parameters are unchanged.
#
# Kernel: 3CX ship a stock Debian 12 (Bookworm) amd64 netboot kernel --
# confirmed 2026-07-27 via `file` against a known snapshot.debian.org Bookworm
# netboot build (exact version string + byte-identical checksum). Since it's
# genuine unmodified Debian, it's filed under debian/bookworm/ rather than
# duplicated here -- same file, same reasoning as debian/trixie/ above just a
# different release. initrd.gz is 3CX's own custom build (checksum + size
# differ from stock Debian) so that one stays under 3cx/.
#
# url= points at this server's own copy of 3CX's preseed rather than
# downloads-global.3cx.com, matching this file's established pattern of
# self-hosting rather than depending on an external download server staying
# up. The preseed file itself is unmodified vendor content -- note its own
# late_command still fetches post-install_12.5.0_a29bc3d.txt live from
# downloads-global.3cx.com during the install (that happens after this kernel
# has already booted, outside iPXE's control; a local copy of that file also
# sits alongside the preseed in 3cx/ if this ever needs to be fully offline).
#
# x86_64 only -- 3CX does not publish an arm64 installer.
# ===========================================================================
:3cx-install
iseq ${arch} arm64 && goto noarch-msg ||
kernel ${boot-url}/debian/bookworm/x86_64/linux vga=788 ipv6.disable=1 auto=true priority=high url=${boot-url}/3cx/preseed_12.5.0_a29bc3d.txt --- quiet THREECXMARKER=DEBIAN-3CX-ISO
initrd ${boot-url}/3cx/initrd.gz
boot

# ===========================================================================
# OPENBSD
# ===========================================================================
:openbsd-auto
set base ${boot-url}/openbsd/7.9/${arch}
echo OpenBSD AUTO (${arch}) from ${base}
kernel ${base}/bsd.rd
boot

:openbsd-auto-s
set base ${boot-url}/openbsd/7.9/${arch}
echo OpenBSD AUTO SERIAL (${arch}) from ${base}
kernel ${base}/bsd.rd
boot

:openbsd-inter-s
set base ${boot-url}/openbsd/7.9/${arch}
echo OpenBSD INTERACTIVE SERIAL (${arch}) from ${base}
kernel ${base}/bsd.rd
boot

# ===========================================================================
# PROXMOX VE
# Not a PXE boot target -- confirmed live, 2026-07-18: the BMC-mounted-ISO +
# PXE-delivered-vmlinuz/initrd.img split (an earlier version of this entry)
# failed its first real test boot outright (blank cursor, installer never
# picked up the mounted ISO). See
# docs/proxmox/pxe-proxmox-autoinstall-build-log.md for the full history.
#
# PVE nodes are built rarely enough that a fully unattended PXE flow was
# solving a harder problem than the actual job needed -- the real, current
# path is simpler: mount a plain, unmodified Proxmox VE ISO via iLO/iDRAC
# virtual media, select "Install Proxmox VE (Automated)" at Proxmox's own
# boot menu with no fetch URL configured (this is documented, expected
# behaviour, not an error -- see docs/bootstrap/bootstrapping.md 6.3), which
# drops to a root shell. From there:
#   wget http://<provisioning-server>/proxmox/select-pve-answer.sh
#   sh select-pve-answer.sh
# does the site/disk-count detection and answer-file fetch that used to be a
# fully manual wget, then hands back to the operator to type `exit` and let
# the installer proceed. See select-pve-answer.sh's own header for the full
# detail -- none of this touches PXE/iPXE at all.
# ===========================================================================
:proxmox-ve
echo Proxmox VE is not built via PXE -- mount a plain Proxmox ISO via iLO/BMC
echo virtual media instead, then run select-pve-answer.sh at the installer's
echo root shell. See docs/proxmox/pxe-proxmox-autoinstall-build-log.md.
prompt Press any key to return to menu...
goto main

# ===========================================================================
# PROXMOX DCM
# ===========================================================================
:proxmox-dcm
echo Proxmox DCM boot not yet configured
prompt Press any key to return to menu...
goto main

# ===========================================================================
# PROJEKT klargoring
# Robert's own hand-rolled live Proxmox installer distro, built separately --
# NOT the Proxmox-official auto-install ISO path that :proxmox-ve's own
# history (see that section's comment, v2.3-v2.5) already tried via PXE and
# abandoned after a failed live test. Different distro, different mechanism.
# Uses ${boot-url}/${site-prefix} like every other entry here rather than a
# hardcoded IP/site -- ${site-prefix}-answer.toml matches the same per-site
# answer-file convention already established above (env_edinburgh/
# env_fredericia set it to VRK/FRD respectively).
#
# ARM64 added 2026-08-06 (Robert, following PVE 9.2's own arm64 release --
# https://pve.proxmox.com/wiki/Roadmap). Unlike debian/gparted/openbsd's own
# per-arch subfolders (identically-named files, ${arch} alone resolves the
# path), klargoring's real GitHub release
# (github.com/knightmare2600/klargoring) publishes asymmetric filenames --
# vmlinuz/installer-initrd.img (unsuffixed) for amd64, vmlinuz-arm64/
# installer-initrd-arm64.img for arm64 -- confirmed against the actual
# klargoring-20260806-trixie-4 release assets, not guessed. Branches to two
# separate labels with fully-literal kernel/initrd paths rather than
# ${arch}-style variable indirection -- three independent indirected
# variables (dir/vmlinuz/initrd name) would let check_bootstrap_assets.py's
# indirection resolver (not per-section/per-branch-scoped) cross-product
# them into 8 combinations instead of the 2 real ones, silently asserting 4
# asset paths iPXE could never actually request. Literal paths sidestep that
# entirely -- each label is exactly what gets requested, nothing to resolve.
# ===========================================================================
:klargoring
iseq ${arch} arm64 && goto klargoring-arm64 || goto klargoring-amd64

:klargoring-amd64
kernel ${boot-url}/proxmox/klargoring/amd64/vmlinuz toml_url=${boot-url}/proxmox/${site-prefix}-answer.toml confirm-wipe no-reboot
initrd ${boot-url}/proxmox/klargoring/amd64/installer-initrd.img
boot

:klargoring-arm64
kernel ${boot-url}/proxmox/klargoring/arm64/vmlinuz-arm64 toml_url=${boot-url}/proxmox/${site-prefix}-answer.toml confirm-wipe no-reboot
initrd ${boot-url}/proxmox/klargoring/arm64/installer-initrd-arm64.img
boot

# ===========================================================================
# GPARTED
# ===========================================================================
:gparted
kernel ${boot-url}/gparted/${arch}/vmlinuz boot=live union=overlay components ip=dhcp noswap fetch=${boot-url}/gparted/${arch}/filesystem.squashfs
initrd ${boot-url}/gparted/${arch}/initrd.img
boot

# ===========================================================================
# WINPE x86_64
# Streamed via wimboot over HTTP -- the WIM is not loaded into RAM in full;
# pages are fetched on demand, keeping memory use low.
# Boots to cmd.exe. VMware PVSCSI/VMXNET3 and VirtIO SCSI/NetKVM drivers
# are injected at build time. Tools are in X:\Windows\Tools\.
#
# wimboot v2.7.1+ extracts BCD and boot.sdi from the WIM automatically so
# no separate bootmgr or BCD file is needed alongside the WIM.
# wimboot v2.9.0+ also extracts boot.stl automatically (Windows 11 24H2).
# Recommend keeping all wimboot binaries at v2.9.0+.
# Ref: https://github.com/ipxe/wimboot/releases
#
# UEFI firmware  -> wimboot     (x86_64 EFI binary)
# BIOS firmware  -> wimboot.i386 (x86 BIOS/32-bit EFI binary)
# Detected via ${buildarch}: x86_64 = UEFI, i386 = BIOS.
#
# Files required on provisioning server under winpe/x86_64/:
#   wimboot        -- EFI binary  (for UEFI firmware VMs)
#   wimboot.i386   -- BIOS binary (for legacy BIOS firmware VMs)
#   boot_x64.wim   -- self-contained WIM from Build-WinPE.ps1
# ===========================================================================
:winpe-x64
iseq ${arch} arm64 && goto noarch-msg ||
iseq ${buildarch} i386 && set winpe-boot ${boot-url}/winpe/x86_64/wimboot.i386 || set winpe-boot ${boot-url}/winpe/x86_64/wimboot
kernel ${winpe-boot}
initrd ${boot-url}/winpe/x86_64/boot_x64.wim  Boot.wim
boot

# ===========================================================================
# WINPE ARM64
# Uses wimboot.arm64 -- AArch64 UEFI binary, added in wimboot v2.8.0.
# Same wimboot streaming pattern as x86_64 -- no UEFI HTTP Boot handoff,
# no separate bootmgr or BCD file needed.
# ARM64 WinPE requires UEFI firmware -- BIOS is not applicable on ARM64.
#
# wimboot v2.9.0+ extracts boot.stl automatically (Windows 11 24H2 support).
# Ref: https://github.com/ipxe/wimboot/releases
#
# This entry is only reachable on ARM64 -- the iseq guard below blocks x86_64.
#
# Files required on provisioning server under winpe/arm64/:
#   wimboot.arm64  -- AArch64 EFI binary (rename from release download)
#   boot_arm64.wim -- self-contained WIM from Build-WinPE.ps1
# ===========================================================================
:winpe-arm64
iseq ${arch} x86_64 && goto noarch-msg ||
kernel ${boot-url}/winpe/arm64/wimboot.arm64
initrd ${boot-url}/winpe/arm64/boot_arm64.wim  Boot.wim
boot

# ===========================================================================
# HDT  (x86_64 only)
# iPXE cannot run .c32 COM32 modules directly -- hdt.c32 (and its
# libcom32.c32/libutil.c32 dependencies) only work under a real SYSLINUX-
# family loader. Chains to lpxelinux.0, not pxelinux.0 -- this server is
# HTTP-only (no TFTP daemon anywhere in this repo), and lpxelinux.0 is
# SYSLINUX's own HTTP-native PXELINUX build, so it can fetch
# hdt/pxelinux.cfg/default (and hdt.c32 itself) the same way iPXE just
# fetched it, over plain HTTP. Plain pxelinux.0 expects TFTP for those
# follow-up fetches and would hang here -- confirmed 2026-07-27 this was
# never going to work as originally written.
# ===========================================================================
:hdt
iseq ${arch} arm64 && goto noarch-msg ||
chain ${boot-url}/hdt/lpxelinux.0

# ===========================================================================
# SYSTEM
# ===========================================================================
:shell
shell

:reboot
reboot

:shutdown
poweroff

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared: architecture not supported message
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
:noarch-msg
echo ${arch} is not supported for this option
prompt Press any key to return to menu...
goto main
